    "description": "Published Sharif University of Technology courses illustrate cybersecurity teaching through network protection and the security of machine-learning systems. Their outlines combine technical analysis with privacy, measurement ethics, and restrictions on misuse, but do not establish the scale of cybersecurity education across Iran.",
    "tags": ["Cybersecurity", "Network security", "Privacy", "Machine learning security", "University education"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:41:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:41:18Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Cybersecurity Education in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented Iranian cybersecurity teaching examples covering network defense, privacy, machine-learning security, and responsible research."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "network-security",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning to reason about network risk",
        "content": "Sharif University of Technology’s published Advanced Network Security course for Fall 2025 describes protection of modern networks at Internet scale. Its opening topics identify assets, trust boundaries, attacker capabilities, and risks to confidentiality, integrity, availability, and privacy. In practical terms, students are asked to consider what needs protection, who might threaten it, and which failures matter. [s1]\n\nThe outline also addresses what defenders can observe when traffic is encrypted. It distinguishes information available from endpoints, networks, and the Internet’s control systems, alongside privacy constraints. Detection topics include signatures, anomalies, and the consequences of false alarms or missed attacks. Transport-security teaching covers TLS and QUIC, protocols used to protect communications, together with certificate issuance, revocation, and transparency. These are documented syllabus topics; the page does not independently demonstrate that every scheduled activity occurred or that students achieved operational proficiency. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "machine-learning-security",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Security of machine-learning systems",
        "content": "A separate Sharif course, Security and Privacy in Machine Learning, is also labeled Fall 2025. It examines vulnerabilities during model training and use, together with methods intended to improve robustness and privacy. Its published schedule includes adversarial examples, poisoning, model extraction, and privacy-related topics. These extend cybersecurity teaching beyond conventional network administration to the behavior of learned systems. [s2]\n\nThe course explicitly addresses the possibility that the technologies studied could infringe others’ rights. Its policy restricts misuse and requires instructor permission where an activity could be perceived as such. This establishes a stated classroom boundary, rather than evidence about compliance outside the course. It is also an institutional course policy, not a complete account of Iranian law. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "teaching-relationship",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Two documented areas of cybersecurity teaching",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Published Sharif security courses\"] -->|\"Includes course example\"| B[\"Advanced Network Security\"]\n  A -->|\"Includes course example\"| C[\"Security and Privacy in Machine Learning\"]\n  B -->|\"Syllabus covers\"| D[\"Network observation and detection\"]\n  B -->|\"Syllabus covers\"| E[\"Secure transport and certificates\"]\n  C -->|\"Syllabus covers\"| F[\"Model vulnerabilities and robustness\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The branches identify subjects in two published course outlines. They do not indicate prerequisites, institutional reporting lines, or a national qualification framework.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "limits-of-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From course descriptions to educational outcomes",
        "content": "These examples show why cybersecurity education involves both technical mechanisms and judgments about evidence and permissible activity. The network-security outline includes consent, data minimization, and retention, while the machine-learning course makes responsible use an explicit condition of participation. The interpretation supported here is limited but useful: the published teaching designs recognize privacy and ethics alongside technical analysis. [s1][s2]\n\nThey cannot establish a national workforce pipeline, the availability of specialist teaching outside Sharif, or the effectiveness of professional training. Nor does the existence of an advanced course show that a beginner can enroll without prior preparation. A complete account needs broader institutional evidence and independent information about practical assessment, access, and graduate destinations.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
